<Project>

  <!-- Repository-wide NuGet dependency audit.

       Ported from the STP engine pipeline, where NuGetAudit is the primary
       dependency control (finding C5). Before this file existed, nothing in
       this repository audited NuGet dependencies on any ref, in any workflow.

       NuGetAuditMode=all is load-bearing, not decoration. It defaults to 'all'
       only for projects targeting net10.0 or higher; every project here targets
       net8.0 or netstandard2.0, so the default is 'direct' - top-level packages
       only. Measured on this repository at 3021286: a High-severity advisory
       (System.Text.Json 6.0.0, GHSA-8g4q-xg66-9fp4, transitive via
       Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console) produced ZERO warnings under the
       default and fired immediately under 'all'.

       NuGetAuditLevel=high matches the engine. Consequence worth knowing: low
       and moderate advisories are then not reported at all, not even as
       warnings. Lower this to 'moderate' or 'low' if that visibility is wanted;
       the WarningsAsErrors line below is what decides the build, not this.

       NU1900 ("error communicating with package source while getting
       vulnerability information") is deliberately left a WARNING, not an error.
       That is the documented behaviour and it is the reason a green restore is
       not by itself proof that an audit happened - an unreachable advisory
       source degrades to silence. Directory.Solution.targets asserts that every
       project was actually audited; that assertion is the mechanical form of
       this caveat. Do not add NU1900 to WarningsAsErrors without deciding what
       a transient nuget.org outage should do to an unrelated build.

       Suppressions: there are none, and that is deliberate. The engine
       suppressed two advisories on the reasoning that the root package "cannot
       move"; a root does not have to move for a transitive to, both had
       published fixes, and the suppression silenced its own gate while leaving
       both advisories in the shipped SBOM. It now carries zero suppressions.
       Prefer bumping the closest package to a direct reference. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <NuGetAudit>true</NuGetAudit>
    <NuGetAuditMode>all</NuGetAuditMode>
    <NuGetAuditLevel>high</NuGetAuditLevel>
    <WarningsAsErrors>$(WarningsAsErrors);NU1903;NU1904</WarningsAsErrors>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
